"""Meetings M15–M22: Weeks 5–8 under D49 — one Studio per week. Monday and
Wednesday teach that studio's book lessons together with their "It is your
turn" work, and Friday IS that studio's milestone; course milestone M(n)
presents book Milestone (n+1).

Week 5 (nb05) — Studio 5, the route hub: Monday runs the three route signals
across five shallow route cards and the route/licence matrix; Wednesday runs
the five-route jigsaw (advocate roles drawn independently of project routes)
and the instructor-assigned mandated contrast. Reading is ROUTE-CONDITIONAL:
your own route's pathway lesson in full plus one assigned contrast; the
hybrid overlay binds only when the two-question stage test returns two yeses.
Friday is M5 = Book Milestone 5, Research Contract v1.

Week 6 (nb06) — Studio 6: Monday governs how data reach you (acquisition
routes keyed to permission status, the provenance record, the primary-source
drill); Wednesday governs what your numbers mean (concept -> construct ->
indicator, reliability on ITEMS, validity as interpretation-and-use) and
settles the Contract v0 provisional operationalization as a numbered version.
Friday is M6 = Book Milestone 6.

Week 7 (nb07) — Studio 7 in ONE week, both lessons: Monday is AI as
programmer (Contract-to-code correspondence, the known-answer test, the line
review); Wednesday is AI as analytical assistant (execute, attach uncertainty,
two independent re-derivations, a licensed placebo, the real clean restart,
the environment record, the claim-to-output trace). Friday m20 is M7 = Book
Milestone 7 AND the FIXED URC abstract internal gate (Fri Oct 9).

Week 8 (nb08) — Studio 8, SHORT WEEK (no class Mon Oct 12, October break):
ONE Wednesday lecture teaches the spine that unifies four lessons — pre-list
before you look, read spans inside commensurable panels, judge negative tests
against the null reference spread rather than 0.00, and adjudicate every flag
against a real check. Friday is M8 = Book Milestone 8, the robustness audit.
Week 8 runs as a SEALED-EXECUTION studio, because four lessons and one lecture
leave no room to write and run an audit on the same Friday: Wednesday's
transfer block ENDS with a frozen, syntax-checked but UNRUN scaffold carrying
the three pre-listed checks, the negative test, and a written prediction for
each; Friday only EXECUTES and adjudicates. The reviewer round is prioritized
rather than exhaustive — ONE flag is verified in class by a check the student
runs, and every remaining flag goes on a pending register with the check that
would settle it, since pending is an honest state and a flag written up as
checked when no check ran is a defect.

Mon/Wed lectures open with the ten-minute LAB MEETING (D74) and are led by the
instructor from minute 10, on the fixed 10/21/12/7 and 10/20/8/4/8 frames;
Fridays are studios on 5/40/5. The lecture's puzzle, retrieval drill, and
challenge are instructor-owned and open the block that starts at minute 10; the
reporter spends seven minutes on a decision from their own project and the
evidence behind it, takes three minutes of questions, teaches nothing, and is
not graded on it. Scored colleague audits run inside Wednesday's
laboratory in Weeks 5, 6, and 8 and are never assigned as homework and never
scheduled on a Friday; Week 7's colleague material is UNSCORED reading inside
the lecture blocks (its clean-but-wrong pipeline and its uncertainty-free result
card), with the instructor's accuracy lock still naming the seeded flaws aloud."""

